# Java/Kotlin + Spring coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to current Spring
Boot 3.x idiom, for both Java and Kotlin sources. Applies only to
`*.java`/`*.kt`/`*.kts` files — everything not Spring/Java/Kotlin-specific
still comes from the common rules this file `extends`.

## Dependency injection

- Prefer constructor injection over field injection: a Java `@Service`
  declares its dependencies as `private final` fields set in the
  constructor (no `@Autowired` needed on the constructor once there is
  only one), or a Kotlin class declares them as constructor `val`
  properties. Field-level `@Autowired` is discouraged — it hides the
  dependency graph, prevents the field from being `final`/`val`, and
  makes the class impossible to construct without the Spring container in
  a plain unit test.
- A class with more than 4-5 constructor dependencies is a signal the
  class has too many responsibilities, not a reason to fall back to field
  injection to make the constructor shorter.

## Layered architecture

- Keep the controller/service/repository layers distinct: a
  `@RestController` parses/validates the request and delegates to a
  `@Service`; business logic and transaction boundaries live in the
  service layer; a `@Repository`/Spring Data interface is the only layer
  that talks to persistence. Do not put query logic in a controller or
  HTTP concerns in a service.
- Expose a request/response DTO at the API boundary, never a JPA `@Entity`
  directly — an entity carries lazy associations and persistence-specific
  fields that leak internal structure and can trigger
  `LazyInitializationException` when serialized outside a transaction.

## Naming and structure

- Java: standard `PascalCase` for types, `camelCase` for members/methods,
  packages lowercase with no underscores.
- Kotlin: `PascalCase` for classes, `camelCase` for functions/properties;
  prefer a `data class` for a DTO/value-holder instead of a plain class
  with manually written `equals`/`hashCode`/`toString`.
- Kotlin null-safety: model an optional field as a nullable type (`String?`)
  and handle it with `?.`/`?:`/`requireNotNull`, not `!!` — a `!!` on
  anything that can plausibly be null at runtime (a request field, a
  lookup result) reintroduces the NPE the type system exists to prevent.

## Bean Validation and DTOs

- Annotate request DTO fields with `jakarta.validation` constraints
  (`@NotNull`, `@NotBlank`, `@Size`, `@Email`, etc.) and mark the
  controller parameter `@Valid`/`@Validated` — validation belongs on the
  DTO at the boundary, not re-implemented as manual `if` checks inside
  the service.

## Formatting

- Follow the project's configured formatter (`spotless`, `ktlint`,
  google-java-format, or whatever `build.gradle(.kts)`/`pom.xml` already
  wires up) rather than hand-formatting around it; do not introduce a
  second formatter into a project that has already standardized on one.
